Interfaceless (@interfaceless: 0, #part-of @interface, #2.0.0) {

We need to model everything, and be able to not interact with Lince to have our needs met. Not necessairly knowing Lince exists, not necessairly continuing the project with the name Lince, quietly disappearing, being absorved by a bigger project, giving it capabilites of Lince. Modeling things in Lince is also a Need. So if i have a Need to model something as big as the observable universe or bigger i should, computers handle scale well, an i128 is enough. Setting a boundary on a part of the universe gives us a sandbox, if we have an Earth globe at our disposal to create these areas we may edit them to escape to fantasy land. I take the place that is my house on the map and put all my Needs and Contributions, relay it as being from my city for privacy. I look at things that are Needed in the areas close to me, or in the other side of the planet, I make transfers with them, of physical resources, information, services... I automate workflows, and I create scenarios on top of visualizations of the real world. I take tools like Gaussian Splatting, photos, videos to see what the world is like in the present, and I overlap it, mesh it together with my assets, the structures I create, using tools like game engines, blender, CAD, Generative World Models to transform the digital representation of the world, taking the real version or the simulated, gamified one and create the Needs and model the Contributions related to it, assign Actors to take care of them. Possibly doing that without interacting with Lince. That's the goal.

For that a system that allows for maximum control of the scenes, is needed, the freedom of Minecraft with real world data and tiny, tiny voxels. That would be the end of the Lince's interface, nothing grater is justified to be built according to the philosophy. It would easily be able to display current web pages, launch other systems and games inside it. You open Lince, it has everything you should Need, and give you freedom to do anything you want in it's digital representation, connected completely with the real world if you want it.

- [ ] Voxel system to go from outer zoom of the observable universe down to atoms (Bevy's Big Space: https://github.com/aevyrie/big_space/tree/bevy-0.19).
- [ ] With the interface that has virtually limitless scale, we focus on centering the experience on the user's interest, Earth's globe as a 3D space with it's real topography, updateable in real time.
- [ ] In any scale, one can take what is there as a point in which the components/widgets/sands/visual tools have at their disposal will be in a size it is good to work with, either locking things to the HUD with pinned sands, or putting them geographically in one region of the space: only in their house when they zoom in the map into their room, or the part of the room in space that is their desk, and inside they will see their widgets. In summary going from 3D to 2D, where you feel more productive with the dimensions you want.
- [ ] Being able to connect the data of parts that are anywhere in the Space.
- [ ] Being able to generate instantly a representation of something you want in any part of the Space, using Fiote (JEPA model?).
- [ ] Analyzing with Fiote what some part of the space we are seeing is, and the possible Needs/Contributions we care about. 
- [ ] View the future simulation of such world based on the possible Automations and Transfers that will happen, as transformations and scenarios of the Space, versions of it, mixed in a same scene, like if 50% transparency, some house that can be built, as if i'm planning a farm in a property and i'm putting down in the simulation where each resource will be, and after setting them, taking the unfinished work, exploding it into each tiny part and Assigning it to contrActors in Lince and doing it myself too.
}
